WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.850
Every actor understands the huge significance
of being cast in a Star Wars movie.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.879
But how do movie stars actually react when
they learn they’re going to play in the

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.620
world’s most famous franchise?

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.960
Here’s how these Star Wars actors found
out they'd been invited to a galaxy far, far

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.460
away.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.740
The story of Mark Hamill's casting as Luke
Skywalker is often made out to be a typical

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.720
Hollywood fairy tale, where the penniless
actor on the verge of giving up on his dreams

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.440
lands the audition of a lifetime.

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:29.820
And while Luke Skywalker certainly was the
role of a lifetime, Hamill was no penniless

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.320
actor.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.040
In fact, he has taken to Twitter in the past
to dispel the myth that he was sleeping on

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.480
his friend Robert Englund's couch when Englund
told him to try for the role.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.020
In fact, Hamill not only had his own one-bedroom
apartment in Malibu at the time, but his agent

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.780
had already set up an audition for him.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.180
Even back in 1977, Hamill was insistent on
how mundane the process really was.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.120
"I auditioned like anyone else…”

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:51.940
“And you got the part?”

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:52.480
“Yeah."

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.180
That audition process started in 1975, as
a joint casting call with Brian De Palma's

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.420
supernatural horror flick Carrie.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.560
Hamill was called back for a screen test with
Harrison Ford in 1976, and the rest is history.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.060
"If the Empire had a new weapon capable of
doing that I’ve had heard about it I’d

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:08.920
know something.”

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.440
“Well now you know.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.260
The enemy is on the move we haven’t much
time."

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.160
Harrison Ford's Star Wars story actually is
a little more of a fairy tale.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.380
In 1975, when casting began, Ford hadn't appeared
in a film with a theatrical release since

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.500
Francis Ford Coppola's The Conversation in
1974.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.600
He'd started working as a carpenter to make
extra money, and it was while he was fixing

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.750
up Coppola's office that he ran into George
Lucas, who'd directed Ford in 1973's American

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:36.240
Graffiti.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.520
Neither thought much about the reunion until
Ford's friend and Lucas' casting agent Fred

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.060
Roos recommended Ford stand in as Han Solo
with actors auditioning for other parts.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.380
According to Ford, he read with over 100 hopefuls,
before being told that he had actually made

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.140
it onto the shortlist himself.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.920
Lucas's version of events seems to back this
up, too.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.670
"You want do this You wanna stand in you wanna
reads some parts against these other parts

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.390
so that we can get through this thing, and
he said yeah and he started reading and he

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.540
read them better than anyone else did."

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.920
Of course, Ford was characteristically matter-of-fact
about landing the job, saying:

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.340
"They asked me if I wanted to do it, and I
said, 'Sure, why not?'"

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.640
While Hamill and Ford were finding plenty
of work but little name recognition, Carrie

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.560
Fisher had nailed the recognition but struggled
to find work.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.020
Her parents, Debbie Reynolds and Eddie Fisher,
had been a Hollywood golden couple and later

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.240
a Hollywood scandal, thanks to Eddie's affair
with Elizabeth Taylor.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.000
Meanwhile, Carrie Fisher had made a brief
appearance in Shampoo in 1975, and at the

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.780
end of that year, while on Christmas break
from drama school, she auditioned for Lucas

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.340
and Carrie director, Brian De Palma.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.820
In her memoir, Fisher recalled that meeting
and being called back to read with Ford.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.920
When her agent called to tell her she'd got
the part, Fisher wrote:

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.740
"He laughed, then I laughed and dropped the
phone and ran out into the front yard and

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:47.620
into the street.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.400
It was raining.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.760
It didn't rain in L.A.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.620
It was raining in L.A. and I was Princess
Leia."

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.880
As happy as she was to become Princess Leia,
however, the role did come with a few drawbacks.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:03.420
"They asked me to lose - at that point - 10
pounds - they thought it was all in my face."

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.900
One person who didn't buy into the hype around
the original Star Wars trilogy was Natalie

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.900
Portman, who'd yet to turn two years old when
Return of the Jedi was released.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.300
She was only 14 when she was offered the role
of Queen Amidala in The Phantom Menace, but

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.590
she'd already found critical acclaim at 13,
first as a trainee hitman in Léon: The Professional

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.680
and then later in 1995's Heat.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.800
On the press tour for The Phantom Menace,
Portman pointed out that she'd never even

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:28.959
seen the other films.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.060
"I hadn't seen the films until it was offered
to me so…

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.340
I wasn’t really aware of what Star Wars
was at all."

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.560
Far from leaping at the chance to be in Star
Wars, Portman took some time to actually consider

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.020
the role before taking it.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.200
"It took me a few weeks to decide but doing
this film it’s a huge commitment to sign

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.500
on for three films when you're fourteen."

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.040
Like Harrison Ford, Samuel L. Jackson didn't
entirely know what he was getting himself

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:56.480
into when he met with George Lucas but as
a long-time Star Wars fanboy, he was totally

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:57.860
on board nonetheless.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.900
The two first got together after Jackson went
on a talk show and mentioned that he'd like

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:02.900
to work with Lucas.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.019
Soon after, Lucas' people got in touch and
arranged a meeting.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:10.300
"He said 'I don't know what you’d do in
it but I’m like look: Make me a stormtrooper.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:12.740
I just wanna run across the screen.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.430
Nobody even needs to know."

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.539
A few months later, Jackson said he got a
call that Lucas was shooting in London, and

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.660
he flew out without even having seen a script.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.720
It was only when he arrived at the costume
fitting and was handed the iconic robes that

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.760
he learned that he was going to be playing
a Jedi.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.920
Jackson also got to choose his own lightsaber
handle, which the prop department inscribed

00:04:30.939 --> 00:04:32.380
with the initials BMF.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:38.680
"Now, I want you to go in that bag, and find
my wallet.”

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.020
“Which one is it?”

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.200
“It’s the one that says bad motherf-----."

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.510
Jackson later said that, despite his character's
unpleasant fate in Revenge of the Sith, he'd

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.980
still happily make more Star Wars movies.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:56.460
Even before being cast as the sequel trilogy's
Force-wielding heroine Rey, Daisy Ridley felt

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.490
like she was in touch with something supernatural.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.999
"And one of them went oh apparently they’re
doing a new Star Wars film.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.300
I literarly my whole body went tingly and
I was like, I think I’m going to get a part

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:06.800
in this film."

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.939
Psychic revelations notwithstanding, it still
took five auditions with director J.J. Abrams

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.680
for Ridley to land the role of Rey.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.699
Still, she was offered the role nonetheless,
and only ran into trouble when it came to

00:05:17.699 --> 00:05:19.080
actually accepting it.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.180
"I checked my voicemail and there was a voicemail
and I was like “oh God Oh God” and I was

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:27.040
like and I didn’t know how to call America,
because I’d never called America, on my

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:27.540
English phone."

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.960
Unfortunately, Ridley's hopes for an immediate
celebration were scuppered.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.860
"And I was outside the theater and the play
had started and it was a f------ long play."

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.820
Before he made six figures playing Finn in
The Force Awakens, John Boyega was only really

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:45.919
known for indie alien thriller Attack the
Block, and his bank account reflected his

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:46.900
acting struggles.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:51.640
In 2018, Boyega told an interviewer that he
literally drained his savings just to get

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.900
a cab ride for his final meeting with Abrams.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:54.740
The good news?

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:55.740
He got the part.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:56.420
The bad news?

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.380
He had no money to get a ride home.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:02.940
Once he finally got home, though, Boyega said
his immediate reaction was one of relief.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:08.240
"I was I was looking forward getting a good
night’s sleep I wasn’t contemplating on

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.560
whether my life was going to change of if
I was going to work on a franchise."

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:15.099
However, not everyone at home fully grasped
the importance of the moment.

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:19.149
"I was like, dad I’ve just been cast in
Star Wars and he was like ‘Oh My God!

00:06:19.149 --> 00:06:20.629
You are in Star Wars?

00:06:20.629 --> 00:06:23.340
That is fantastic I knew it!

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:24.760
What is Star Wars?’"

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:30.139
Before he was cast in Star Wars, Oscar Isaac
was an indie darling who'd appeared in a number

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:34.110
of low budget movies that performed well both
with critics and at the box office.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:39.039
For example, he showed up in Drive and Ex
Machina, and played the lead in the Coen brothers'

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.440
Inside Llewyn Davis.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.999
However, when Isaac was offered the opportunity
to star in The Force Awakens, director J.J.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.800
Abrams had to convince him to sign up for
the Star Wars role.

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:53.160
Abrams brought Isaac to Paris in 2014 for
a meeting at coffee shop and celebrity hangout

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.120
Café de Flore.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:58.180
And while they were there, Abrams revealed
a lot of key details about the project.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.780
As Isaac explained to Vulture:

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:02.559
"He basically told me the whole thing, the
whole story.

00:07:02.559 --> 00:07:05.149
It was incredibly exciting just to be called
in and told that.

00:07:05.149 --> 00:07:05.920
It was awesome."

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:11.199
But Isaac was less enthusiastic about playing
the skilled yet rebellious pilot Poe Dameron,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:11.940
telling GQ:

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.920
"I didn't know if I could make it interesting."

00:07:14.149 --> 00:07:18.260
After a few days thinking it over, he signed
up, but wasn't necessarily fulfilled by the

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:19.300
role, explaining:

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.020
"I actually felt the most green and insecure
that I had in a long time.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.460
[…] It felt weird and like I was not being
creative."

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.719
Of course, the experience wasn't enough to
put him off blockbuster franchises in 2016,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.620
he played the eponymous villain in X-Men:
Apocalypse.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:39.780
Adam Driver was busy filming HBO's critically-acclaimed
series Girls when he got the call about Star

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.280
Wars.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.820
And Driver's road to the role of Kylo Ren
wasn't exactly run-of-the-mill.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:48.640
In fact, Driver clarified in 2016 that he
never even auditioned for the role.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:52.740
Driver has also said that all that waiting
meant it took a while for it to hit him that

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.920
he wasn't just preparing for any old film
but a Star Wars film.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:00.219
In the end, it wasn't until he found himself
surrounded by the movie's costumes that he

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:01.380
realized what he was in for.

00:08:01.469 --> 00:08:05.839
"The new characters and stormtroopers the
new updated characters that was the first

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:08.080
time when I was like oh oh my God This is
Star Wars."

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:12.319
While a few stars might have professed to
not being huge Star Wars fans before they

00:08:12.319 --> 00:08:16.199
took their roles in the franchise, Gwendoline
Christie was all in on the series.

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:21.290
"I'd wanted to be in Star Wars my whole life
you know I’ve been a fan since I was 6 years

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:22.840
old when I first saw the films."

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:26.529
The Game of Thrones actress said she pestered
her representatives constantly.

00:08:26.529 --> 00:08:33.320
"I was just constantly going on and on about
it and just every phone call everything I’d

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.800
go just oh they’re interested in you in
this and I’d go I want to be in Star Wars."

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:40.099
Like Adam Driver and Samuel L. Jackson, Christie
said that it really hit her that she'd made

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.120
it when she got to put on Captain Phasma's
metal armor and cape.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:48.050
Of course, pre-Phasma, her favourite character
was Leia, and she also majorly fangirled over

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:49.580
meeting Carrie Fisher.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:57.480
"She's different she’s unafraid, she’s
smart, she’s brave, she’s bold, she’s

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:58.860
unashamedly herself."

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.060
Domhnall Gleeson learned that he'd landed
the role of General Hux the day before The

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.420
Force Awakens' now-iconic first table-read.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:11.020
In January 2016, shortly after The Force Awakens
was released, Gleeson went into detail about

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:12.880
actually taking the role of Hux.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:19.700
"They said he wanted power and he was intent
on getting it and he had cool speech."

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.180
Evidently, that pitch totally convinced Gleeson.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:26.620
"You want to do stuff different than you’ve
done before and you want to be part of things

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:27.770
which are really good."

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:31.570
But Gleeson did have some misgivings about
the fame the role would doubtless bring him.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:32.300
He explained:

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.620
"Fame is the end goal for a lot of people,
but it is not mine.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:39.320
I worried that the accompanying fame would
get in the way of how I live my life.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:42.440
[…] It got a little crazy for a couple of
months … and then it died down."

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:48.060
Felicity Jones wasn't quite built for battle
when she was cast as Rogue One's street smart

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.120
rebel Jyn Erso.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:53.280
In fact, she was most famous for playing Jane
Hawking in the Stephen Hawking biopic The

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.300
Theory of Everything, which had earned her
an Oscar nomination — but few fighting skills.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:03.880
"My immediate reaction was that I have to
do a hell of a lot of fitness and fight training

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:07.300
in order to play this incredible leading character."

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.780
Jones credits her agent with introducing her
to the role, telling People:

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.140
"My agent said there's a fantastic leading
female role in the upcoming Star Wars that

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:16.560
I think you're really going to love.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.440
That was my first encounter with Jyn."

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:24.160
In typical top secret Star Wars fashion, Jones
met Rogue One director Gareth Edwards at 5:30

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:27.740
in the morning to discuss the project, and
Jones said she knew she had to take the part

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:28.760
when she read the script.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:29.500
As she put it:

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.680
"It's one of those once-in-a-lifetime experiences
when you read a role like that.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.640
I just was like, I have to do this."

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:40.040
Even after taking the role, it took a little
while for the enormity of it all to sink in.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:43.740
But Jones loved working on the film… even
the intense battle sequences.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:45.180
As the actress explained:

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.640
"There is much joy in being part of it."

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:53.000
Before she landed the role of Rose in The
Last Jedi, Kelly Marie Tran was working full-time

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:57.270
in a temp agency by day, occasionally fitting
in auditions when they came up, and writing

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:58.960
comedy sketches by night.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:02.740
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,
she admitted the whole process was beginning

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:03.660
to wear her down.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:04.420
She explained:

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.350
"When I turned 25, I had been at it for some
years and I was struggling to pay my bills.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:10.100
I was tired.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.740
[…] I would get up at 5 AM and then I wouldn't
be home until 11 PM.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.480
The days were like that for years."

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:19.430
The auditions for The Last Jedi took five
months, which sadly meant that quitting her

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:20.900
day job wasn't an option.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.900
In fact, on the fateful day that she learned
she'd been cast in the film, she had to meet

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:26.980
with director Rian Johnson on her lunch break.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:30.840
And of course, once she found out that she
had the part, she had to keep it a secret.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:31.900
Tran explained:

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.120
"So I went back to work, and answered phones
and answered emails, like nothing happened.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.280
It was the most bizarre experience.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:39.700
Inside I was like, 'Ugh!

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:40.440
Crazy!'"

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.680
By far the cast's biggest Star Wars nerd,
Laura Dern has admitted that being in Star

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:47.600
Wars was pretty much the dream of a lifetime.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:51.880
"When I was a little girl about two blocks
from here at the Cinorama Dome was the first

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:55.160
time I ever waited in line to see a movie
being Star Wars."

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.840
But when director Rian Johnson first pitched
Dern the idea, the exact nature of the role

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:00.860
didn't quite register at first.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.540
"Where is it in small town America or is it,
no no no.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:05.960
It’s Star Wars.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:09.240
I was like: you’re F------ kidding me I'm
sorry."

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.760
For a while, Dern thought he was speaking
metaphorically about her character, until

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:16.460
Johnson finally admitted that he'd called
her in to talk about Star Wars.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.010
But once she got to set, Dern felt the full
force of her new role.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:24.740
"I got to set and they rolled camera and I
opened my eyes and they called action and

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:29.000
I was just staring at C-3PO and I started
to cry."

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.680
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.140
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
